      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x-{x}^{2}}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $g(x)=x-{x}^{2}$ and $f(g(x))=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{g}}=-4{g}^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =-4({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=\\frac{2}{{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(x-{x}^{2})^{′}=1-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (1-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2-4x}{\\sqrt{(x-{x}^{2})^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
